{
  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a strong downtrend (e.g., Example 1) followed by a forecast of steady upward drift, the true outcome often exhibits an immediate sharp spike above the forecast before correcting downward; apply a positive bias correction to the first 3-5 days of the forecast horizon.",
    "If sentiment history contains isolated high values (1.0 or 0.25) amidst low/negative sentiment, the model tends to underestimate the volatility and magnitude of subsequent price moves; increase forecast variance and add a mean-reversion bias toward historical averages after the initial spike.",
    "The model consistently underestimates short-term volatility in volatile regimes (Examples 1-3), producing overly smooth curves that fail to capture sudden reversals; correct by applying a stochastic shock factor to the first week of the forecast when recent standard deviation exceeds 5%.",
    "In periods where sentiment is predominantly negative or neutral with no clear trend signal, the model's linear extrapolation leads to significant overshoots in the middle of the horizon; reduce the slope of the forecast curve and weight recent historical lows more heavily."
  ]
}
